WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:01.810
- [Instructor] In this
video, we're gonna talk

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:04.620
about a concept in chemistry
that's quite important,

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.060
known as dilutions.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.170
So let's do an example.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.610
So let's say we have a large
vat, as much as we need.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:16.730
It's a one-molar solution
of sodium sulfate,

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:17.950
and it's an aqueous solution.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:20.760
So sodium sulfate is dissolved in water.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.020
And let's say we also have
as much water as we need,

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.890
and what we want to do
is create a solution,

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.750
another aqueous solution
of sodium sulfate,

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.420
but one that has a
different concentration,

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.040
in this case, one that
has a lower concentration.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.750
So we want to create
a 0.125-molar solution

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:45.230
of sodium sulfate, and
we want 500 milliliters

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.230
of this new solution.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.820
Pause this video and think about

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.220
how you would approach that.

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.430
All right, now let's
think about this together.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.890
So, first let's just
go over the intuition.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.010
You have a higher concentration here.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.130
You have a lower concentration here.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.070
So our intuition would tell us

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:05.330
is that we're going to take
less than 500 milliliters

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:09.240
of our original solution,
pour some of that in.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.960
That's going to have a
sufficient number of moles

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.690
of sodium sulfate that,

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:18.160
if we were to then fill
this up to 500 milliliters,

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.770
that we would then have
a 0.125-molar solution.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.950
So the question really
is, is how much of this

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.460
do we have to put in, which
we can then dilute with water

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.730
to get to our goal solution?

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:37.290
Well, to answer that question,
we just have to figure out

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:40.080
how many moles of sodium sulfate

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.350
need to be in this final
goal solution, this one

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.630
or this one, depending
on how we visualize it?

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.060
And then, how much of
our original solution,

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.340
of our one-molar solution,
do we need to take out

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.630
to have that many moles?

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.930
And to think about how many moles,

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:58.690
we just have to remind
ourselves what molarity is.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:03.690
We know already that molarity
is equal to number of moles,

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:08.620
number of moles, of solute

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:13.980
per liters of solution,

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.213
liters of solution.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.370
Or another way to think about it is,

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.760
if we multiply both sides
by liters of solution,

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:27.550
we would get liters of solution

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:33.893
times molarity, times molarity,

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.740
is equal to the number of moles of solute,

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:42.740
number of moles of solute.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.560
So what we can do is say,
all right, how many moles

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.100
of our solute do we need in our goal?

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.100
Well, to do that, we just
have to say, all right,

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.700
we want to eventually have
500 milliliters of solution,

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.400
or we could rewrite that

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.207
as 0.500 liters,

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.140
and this little decimal
point right over here

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.110
makes it clear that we're dealing

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:10.310
with three significant figures,

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.340
that we've rounded to the nearest one,

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.060
when we got to this, when we
have this goal right over here,

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.660
or we would round to the nearest,

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:18.410
to the ones place, I guess.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:21.640
So, our goal is to have half a liter

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.060
of solution at a molarity

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.750
of 0.125 molar,

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:29.890
and then that is gonna give us

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:31.270
the number of moles we need.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:33.560
And, if we multiply this
out, this is going to be

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.630
zero point, let's see, half of 12 is 6

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:39.810
and then half of 50 is 25,

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:44.810
0.0625 moles,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.923
moles of solute.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:54.080
And, in this case, our
solute is sodium sulfate.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.330
And let's see if I got the
significant figures right.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:58.520
I have three right over here,

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.560
one, two, three, one, two, three.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:01.580
So I take the product.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:05.060
I'd still have one, two,
three significant figures.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:06.300
So this is our goal.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.290
We want to have this many moles of solute.

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:09.390
So we just have to figure out

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.250
how much of our original
solution do we need

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.650
in order to have that many
moles of sodium sulfate?

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:17.360
So, one way to think about it is,

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.530
there's some mystery volume
of our original solution

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:23.930
we need, and we know what
its concentration is.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.120
It's a one-molar concentration that,

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:28.450
when I take this product,

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:32.920
I am going to get 0.0625 moles

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.400
of sodium sulfate.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.840
And the math here is
pretty straightforward.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.930
We can divide both sides by one molar,

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.830
and what are we going to get?

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.060
And the units work out

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:48.980
because we're in moles
where you have molar here.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:51.240
And so this is going to give
us our answer in liters.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:53.280
You divide both sides by one molar.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:58.280
You're going to get that
question mark is equal to

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:03.670
0.0625 liters of solution.

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:07.330
Or another way to think about
it is, this is equivalent

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:12.100
to 62.5 milliliters

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.930
of our original solution.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:17.300
I want to make sure I got all
the significant figures right.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.620
Had three over there. One,
two, three, one, two, three.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:21.860
And so, yes, right over here.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:24.670
I can still have one, two,
three significant figures

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:26.480
or sometimes called significant digits.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:28.390
And so there we've answered our question.

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:31.090
What I would do is I would take 62.5,

00:05:32.404 --> 00:05:36.020
62.5 milliliters of my original solution,

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:37.550
so that's this over here,

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:40.940
and then I would take my
water and then keep filling

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:45.280
until I get to 500
milliliters, and we're done.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:48.930
At that point, I'm going
to have a 0.125 molar

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.823
of sodium sulfate aqueous solution.

